  "text": "Let's reason step by step to solve the problem.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11} = \\frac{m\\sqrt{n} - p}{q} \\)\n- The area of \\( A_iA_1A_{i+1} \\) is 1 for each \\( 2 \\leq i \\leq 10 \\).\n- \\( \\cos(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = \\frac{12}{13} \\) for each \\( 2 \\leq i \\leq 10 \\).\n- The perimeter of \\( A_1A_2 \\ldots A_{11} \\) is 20.\n\nWe need to find \\( m + n + p + q \\).\n\nFirst, let's consider the area and the cosine of the angles. The area of each triangle \\( A_iA_1A_{i+1} \\) is 1, and the cosine of the angle between these triangles is \\( \\frac{12}{13} \\).\n\nLet's denote the sides of the triangles as follows:\n- \\( A_1A_2 = a_1 \\)\n- \\( A_1A_3 = a_2 \\)\n- \\( \\ldots \\)\n- \\( A_1A_{11} = a_{10} \\)\n\nThe area of each triangle can be expressed using the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot A_1A_i \\cdot A_1A_{i+1} \\cdot \\sin(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) \\]\n\nGiven that \\( \\cos(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = \\frac{12}{13} \\), we can find \\( \\sin(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) \\) using the Pythagorean identity:\n\\[ \\sin(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1}) = \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2(\\angle A_iA_1A_{i+1})} = \\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}} = \\frac{5}{13} \\]\n\nSo, the area of each triangle is:\n\\[ 1 = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot A_1A_i \\cdot A_1A_{i+1} \\cdot \\frac{5}{13} \\]\n\\[ 1 = \\frac{5}{26} \\cdot A_1A_i \\cdot A_1A_{i+1} \\]\n\\[ A_1A_i \\cdot A_1A_{i+1} = \\frac{26}{5} \\]\n\nNow, let's consider the perimeter:\n\\[ A_1A_2 + A_1A_3 + \\ldots + A_1A_{11} = 20 \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11} \\). Let's denote:\n\\[ A_1A_2 = a_1 \\]\n\\[ A_1A_{11} = a_{10} \\]\n\\[ A_1A_3 = a_2 \\]\n\\[ \\ldots \\]\n\\[ A_1A_{10} = a_9 \\]\n\nFrom the area condition, we have:\n\\[ a_1 \\cdot a_2 = \\frac{26}{5} \\]\n\\[ a_2 \\cdot a_3 = \\frac{26}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\ldots \\]\n\\[ a_9 \\cdot a_{10} = \\frac{26}{5} \\]\n\nAnd from the perimeter condition:\n\\[ a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_{10} = 20 \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( a_1 + a_{10} \\). Let's assume \\( a_1 = x \\) and \\( a_{10} = y \\). Then:\n\\[ x \\cdot a_2 = \\frac{26}{5} \\]\n\\[ a_2 \\cdot a_3 = \\frac{26}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\ldots \\]\n\\[ a_9 \\cdot y = \\frac{26}{5} \\]\n\nFrom these equations, we can see that:\n\\[ a_2 = \\frac{26}{5x} \\]\n\\[ a_3 = \\frac{26}{5a_2} = \\frac{26}{5 \\cdot \\frac{26}{5x}} = x \\]\n\\[ a_4 = \\frac{26}{5a_3} = x \\]\n\\[ \\ldots \\]\n\\[ a_9 = x \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{26}{5a_9} = \\frac{26}{5x} \\]\n\nSo, \\( a_1 + a_{10} = x + \\frac{26}{5x} \\).\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we need to consider the perimeter condition:\n\\[ a_1 + a_2 + \\ldots + a_{10} = 20 \\]\n\\[ x + \\frac{26}{5x} + 8x = 20 \\]\n\\[ 9x + \\frac{26}{5x} = 20 \\]\n\\[ 45x^2 + 26 = 100x \\]\n\\[ 45x^2 - 100x + 26 = 0 \\]\n\nSolving this quadratic equation:\n\\[ x = \\frac{100 \\pm \\sqrt{100^2 - 4 \\cdot 45 \\cdot 26}}{2 \\cdot 45} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{100 \\pm \\sqrt{10000 - 4680}}{90} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{100 \\pm \\sqrt{5320}}{90} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{100 \\pm 2\\sqrt{1330}}{90} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{50 \\pm \\sqrt{1330}}{45} \\]\n\nSince \\( x \\) must be positive, we take the positive root:\n\\[ x = \\frac{50 + \\sqrt{1330}}{45} \\]\n\nNow, we can find \\( a_1 + a_{10} \\):\n\\[ a_1 + a_{10} = x + \\frac{26}{5x} = \\frac{50 + \\sqrt{1330}}{45} + \\frac{26}{5 \\cdot \\frac{50 + \\sqrt{1330}}{45}} \\]\n\\[ a_1 + a_{10} = \\frac{50 + \\sqrt{1330}}{45} + \\frac{26 \\cdot 45}{5(50 + \\sqrt{1330})} \\]\n\\[ a_1 + a_{10} = \\frac{50 + \\sqrt{1330}}{45} + \\frac{234}{50 + \\sqrt{1330}} \\]\n\\[ a_1 + a_{10} = \\frac{50 + \\sqrt{1330} + 234}{45} \\]\n\\[ a_1 + a_{10} = \\frac{284 + \\sqrt{1330}}{45} \\]\n\nSo, \\( A_1A_2 + A_1A_{11} = \\frac{284 + \\sqrt{1330}}{45} \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( m = 284 \\), \\( n = 1330 \\), \\( p = 0 \\), and \\( q = 45 \\).\n\nFinally, \\( m + n + p + q = 284 + 1330 + 0 + 45 = 1659 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1659}\n\\]",
